WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.720
Some months ago, we published a Biographics
on Italian WWII navy commander Junio Valerio

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.720
Borghese.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.620
Many of you asked us to cover more military
protagonists from the Italian side of the

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.240
Axis alliance, as they get less attention
than their German counterparts.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:19.269
Here we are today with someone who we feel
may satisfy your curiosity.

00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:23.920
Today’s protagonist was an Olympics-level
horse rider, a ladies’ man, a cavalry officer

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.520
of three wars, a guerrilla fighter, and a
secret agent.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.050
This is the story of how Baron Amedeo Guillet
became the ‘Devil Commander’ who waged

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:39.090
a private war against the British Empire in
East Africa.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:49.820
The Early Wars of Baron Guillet
Baron Amedeo Guillet, was born on February

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.410
7th, 1909 in Piacenza, northern Italy, from
an aristocratic family.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.010
Following in the footsteps of his family’s
tradition, Amedeo enlisted at the military

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:02.830
academy, graduating in 1931 as a 2nd Lieutenant
in the Cavalry.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:06.620
Assigned to a Light Horsemen regiment, he
impressed his superiors with his horse riding

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.800
skills, so much so that he joined the Italian
horse riding team for the upcoming Berlin

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.970
Olympics of 1936.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:18.020
As a young and dashing officer with a perfect
‘stache, Amedeo became renowned also for

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.370
other successes: ladies could not resist him,
and he was rumoured to have dated Hollywood

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.520
actress Mary Pickford, among other celebrities.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.680
But Olympic training and romantic affairs
would soon give way to another calling.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:33.770
In the Summer of 1935, Guillet was ordered
to the Italian colony of Libya, to train a

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.430
squadron of Spahis, local mounted troops.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:41.100
Guillet soon realised that these riders, whom
he described as ‘splendid’, needed little

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.100
training.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.020
In fact, he had a lot to learn from them.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.960
It is not clear whether Guillet knew he was
going to lead the Spahis into battle very

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.960
soon: the war with Abyssinia was around the
corner.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.060
What he did realise, though, is that to properly
lead them and earn their respect, he had to

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.070
speak their language.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.340
Guillet decided to learn Arabic by joining
a local Quranic school, alongside children

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.420
less than half his age.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.260
As it turned out, Guillet had a talent for
learning languages, and very soon he was able

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.870
to communicate with his soldiers.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.759
He would perfect his Arabic in the following
years, an ability that would eventually prove

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:11.759
handy.

00:02:11.759 --> 00:02:17.340
In October 1935, Mussolini ordered the invasion
of Abyssinia, following a border incident

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.400
with Eritrea, an Italian colony.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.760
Emperor Hailé Selassié denounced the act
of aggression to the League of Nations, but

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.840
it resulted in little action or support from
the Western powers, save for an ineffectual

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.760
trade embargo against Italy.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.790
With the start of the war, Guillet could have
asked for a relocation to the motherland,

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:39.300
thanks to his scintillating credentials, but
he asked instead to be assigned to the front.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.760
After two months of little action, it was
time for Guillet and his Spahis to engage

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.500
the enemy at the Battle of Selaclacla, on
Christmas Eve 1935.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.870
At that time, he was suffering from a bad
bout of malaria -- he was so drowsy and feverish

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.290
that he had to rely on his Spahi sergeant
to spot incoming enemies on his behalf.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:00.410
This is how he took notice of an incoming
Ethiopian rifleman, whom he shot with his

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.569
pistol, his first kill in combat.

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:07.069
The battle soon turned into a melee, and another
Ethiopian tried to tackle him off his horse.

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:10.610
Guillet struck him on the head with the hilt
of his sword, knocking him out cold.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:14.709
Immediately afterward, a bullet came flying
toward his stomach; fortunately for him, it

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:18.120
hit the pommel of his saddle and ricocheted
through his left hand.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.020
As the war dragged into 1936, Guillet was
able to experience first-hand the horrors

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.090
and iniquities of modern colonial warfare.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.550
Official historical sources still differ on
whether the Italian Air Force dropped poison

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:36.250
gas canisters on Haile Selassie’s troops
or not, but we have Guillet’s personal,

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:37.590
eye witness account.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.650
He did witness at least one such aerial gas
attacks during the campaign.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.770
Not only did he find it a dishonourable and
inhumane way to conduct an offensive, but

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:50.239
he also observed that in most cases, it was
ineffectual against an unentrenched, highly

00:03:50.239 --> 00:03:52.190
mobile enemy such as the Ethiopians.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.970
Guillet found his own way of fighting to be
the most honourable and effective: a horse,

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.620
a sword, a pistol.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:03:59.690
And a perfectly trimmed moustache.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:05.670
When on the May 5th, 1936 the Italians conquered
the Ethiopian capital of Addis Ababa, Guillet

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.819
was not there to celebrate -- he had been
ordered back to Tripoli for a delicate assignment.

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:13.670
The Duce was due to visit Tripoli, as the
colonial authorities needed a skilled horse

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:18.109
trainer for an elaborate propaganda stunt:
the ceremony of the ‘Sword of Islam’.

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:22.349
After reviewing Italian and colonial troops,
atop a splendid white horse, Mussolini would

00:04:22.349 --> 00:04:26.020
solemnly unsheathe a scimitar for the benefit
of newsreels.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:30.319
The ceremony‘s meaning was to portray Italians
in Africa as natural allies of the Muslim

00:04:30.319 --> 00:04:33.050
populations against other colonial powers.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:37.270
The ceremony was a success, but Guillet’s
hand wound got infected and worsened over

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.150
time, so he was sent to Naples to undergo
surgery.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.740
After the operation, Guillet spent time recovering
with his relatives, the Gandolfos.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:48.610
Among them, his childhood best friend and
favourite cousin, Beatrice.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.110
People at that time should have known better
than to leave an aristocrat and a cousin alone

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:58.759
in the same room: it very often led to much
beseeching, swooning, hand kissing and other

00:04:58.759 --> 00:05:00.219
euphemistic activities.

00:05:00.219 --> 00:05:04.270
The only thing that stood between the union
of Amedeo’s and Beatrice’s trembling lips

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.749
was a pair of expertly groomed whiskers.

00:05:06.749 --> 00:05:11.069
But those, I am sure, were more of a facilitator
than an obstacle.

00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:13.460
But hey, I am not judging: different times,
different habits.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:18.360
Irreverent jokes aside, Amedeo and Beatrice
fell deeply and sincerely in love.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.800
It wouldn’t take long for Amedeo to propose.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.050
On one hand, he would have much loved to speed
up proceedings, but he would not marry her

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.050
yet.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.919
A recent law forbade promotions for unmarried
officers.

00:05:28.919 --> 00:05:33.219
This was meant as an incentive for young men
to marry and reproduce for the growth of the

00:05:33.219 --> 00:05:34.219
nation.

00:05:34.219 --> 00:05:35.530
Guillet was well-aware of the law.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:39.559
He stated that his sense of honour prevented
him from marrying Beatrice straight away -- he

00:05:39.559 --> 00:05:42.919
knew that a promotion to 1st Lieutenant would
follow suit immediately.

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:46.229
And it would look as though he was marrying
her only for that reason!

00:05:46.229 --> 00:05:49.650
His plan B was to seek another war and earn
a promotion on the battlefield.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.840
Only then, he would marry Beatrice.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.759
That seems a total rational way to plan a
wedding.

00:05:54.759 --> 00:05:57.720
The occasion came with the start of Civil
War in Spain.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.219
Fascist Italy and Nazi Germany participated
in that conflict by providing troops and equipment

00:06:02.219 --> 00:06:06.599
to the Nationalist side led by Generalisimo
Francisco Franco.

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:11.990
And so, in the summer of 1937, Guillet volunteered
to lead a detachment of Italian ‘Arditi’,

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:13.259
or storm troops.

00:06:13.259 --> 00:06:17.740
I should specify at this point that Guillet
was never a full supporter of Fascism.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.990
All his military exploits were, and would
always be, related to a sense of duty toward

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:27.120
the King Victor Emmanuel III and the ruling
House of Savoy.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.129
His participation in the Spanish Civil War
was borne of personal considerations, although

00:06:31.129 --> 00:06:35.499
he later declared that he could not stand
the idea of a Communist Spain.

00:06:35.499 --> 00:06:37.990
He soon got in the thick of the action.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:41.730
Assigned to an Italian division known as “The
Black Flames”, he participated in the Battle

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.479
of Santander, August 1937.

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:48.229
This was part of Franco’s campaign to crush
Republican resistance in the North.

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:52.349
Guillet led his men to the assault on the
stronghold of San Pedro.

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:57.430
Despite stiff resistance, Amedeo succeeded
in storming the Republicans, and this opened

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.069
the doors to the advance on Santander.

00:07:00.069 --> 00:07:02.719
The next big battle was Teruel, in the North
West.

00:07:02.719 --> 00:07:06.119
In this occasion Guillet led a squadron of
Moroccan cavalrymen.

00:07:06.119 --> 00:07:10.099
On an assault against a motorised column,
he suffered serious shrapnel wounds, but he

00:07:10.099 --> 00:07:14.349
gritted his teeth and dispersed the enemy
unit, succeeding in capturing three Russian

00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:16.179
armoured cars and their crews.

00:07:16.179 --> 00:07:20.589
After eight months he had earned his battlefield
promotion and a military decoration from Franco

00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:21.589
himself.

00:07:21.589 --> 00:07:25.479
But he had also received a bad leg wound,
meaning his Spanish adventure was over.

00:07:25.479 --> 00:07:29.509
While recovering in a military hospital in
Tripoli, he reflected on the atrocities he

00:07:29.509 --> 00:07:31.740
had witnessed on both sides of the conflict.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.740
In particular, he was very vocal with his
colleagues about the deplorable conduct of

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:36.740
the Germans.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:40.990
At the end of 1938, Guillet had become good
friends with a Libyan girl, a young medical

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:42.939
student he had met at the hospital.

00:07:42.939 --> 00:07:46.680
She was looking forward to her graduation
but there was a problem: she was Jewish, and

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:50.869
the recently approved Racial Laws in Italy
demanded for her to leave University.

00:07:50.869 --> 00:07:56.029
These laws were a consequence of the increasingly
tight bond between Rome and Berlin, as Italian

00:07:56.029 --> 00:07:59.849
Fascism did not have an anti-Semitic component
at its inception.

00:07:59.849 --> 00:08:05.210
Guillet appealed to the Governor of Libya,
Air Marshal Italo Balbo, but all was in vain.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:11.300
That was another blow dealt to Guillet’s
shaky confidence in Mussolini’s government.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:18.169
The Devil Commander
With growing disgust for the political developments

00:08:18.169 --> 00:08:21.740
in Rome, Guillet asked for his next posting
to be in Africa.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:26.229
Luckily for him, in 1939 a family acquaintance
had been appointed as the new Viceroy for

00:08:26.229 --> 00:08:27.909
the East African colonies.

00:08:27.909 --> 00:08:32.070
This was his namesake Amedeo, Duke of Aosta,
a cousin of the King.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:37.250
He had been appointed viceroy with the difficult
task of pacifying Abyssinia after the conquest.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:42.070
Ethiopian guerrilla fighters, loyal to Emperor
Hailé Selassié, were still very active in

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:46.350
1939 and the Duke of Aosta thought men like
Guillet were the answer.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:50.610
So, our protagonist was given command of a
unit called the “Group of Amhara bands,”

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:55.941
a multi-ethnic cavalry force which included
Ethiopian, Eritrean and Yemeni soldiers, led

00:08:55.941 --> 00:08:57.540
by Italian officers.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:02.020
Guillet and the ‘Amharas’ were given total
autonomy and freedom of movement to eradicate

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.390
guerrillas which had now spread into Eritrea.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:09.890
It was during one of these actions that the
Amharas dubbed their leader ‘The Devil Commander’.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:14.519
After a long series of skirmishes with the
guerrillas in the Dougur Duba region, Guillet

00:09:14.519 --> 00:09:18.140
succeeded in luring them into a clash in an
open, flat territory, where he knew his horsemen

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.440
would be at an advantage.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:23.970
Lieutenant Guillet led a charge against a
group of enemies: the attack was successful,

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.330
but his horse was shot from under him.

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.530
Guillet immediately requested a second horse
and charged again.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:35.330
Once more, another enemy formation was routed,
once more, the poor horse was shot down, but

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:36.330
Guillet was unscathed.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:40.510
We like to think at this point that all spare
horses now looked at each other, nodded, and

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:42.850
decided to quietly leave the battlefield unnoticed.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:46.700
While they were doing that, Guillet seized
control of a machine gun position, turned

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:50.360
it towards the remaining rebels and wiped
them out single-handedly.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:53.680
As a result of this action, Guillet’s superiors
awarded him the Silver Medal for Military

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:56.850
Valour – the 2nd highest military decoration
in the Italian Army.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:02.230
Guillet’s colonial troops instead bestowed
on him a higher honour: the nickname of “Devil

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:06.410
Commander,” indicative of his fiery fighting
spirit and apparent immortality.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:09.780
In later life, when recounting this event,
Guillet defined himself as

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.410
“the luckiest man I have ever known”.

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.650
I am sure his horses begged to disagree.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:20.780
The last charge
In early 1940, Guillet made friends with an

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:25.720
Ethiopian Muslim chieftain, in his efforts
to pacify Eritrea and Northern Abyssinia.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.840
Generally, Muslim locals tended to side with
the Italians, while Christians were staunch

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.730
supporters of the Negus -- the Ethiopian leader.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:36.810
Lieutenant Amedeo also became friends with
the young, intelligent and beautiful daughter

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.550
of the chieftain, Khadija.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.210
When Guillet asked her what did she want to
do in life, she replied

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:44.600
‘I am going to marry a chief and be at his
side’

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.020
But she had not chosen the chief yet.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:51.020
When Guillet left the village with his Bands,
he was surprised to see Khadija riding out

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:52.020
to meet him.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:53.250
‘I made my choice,’ she said.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:55.680
‘You are the chief I want to be with!’

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:59.890
Guillet’s initial resistance was futile
and Khadija soon became his lover.

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:04.660
Amedeo’s soul was understandably torn between
the beautiful Ethiopian and Beatrice back

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:05.660
home.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:09.860
When, on the 10th of June 1940 Mussolini declared
war on France and the United Kingdom, the

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:13.620
whole of Italian East Africa found itself
isolated from the motherland.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:17.740
With little hope of seeing Beatrice again,
Amedeo deepened his relationship with Khadija,

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:21.110
who would follow him on the front lines in
many occasions.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:24.960
The Duke of Aosta’s war against the British
and the French in East Africa started well,

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.940
with his troops occupying British Somaliland
and border territories with Kenya and Sudan.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:34.830
However, total isolation meant lack of supplies,
including fuel for tanks and planes.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:39.020
Very soon Aosta had to employ a defensive
strategy: fortifying mountain top redoubts

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:41.060
to resist Allied advance as long as possible.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:45.880
One of these strongholds was Keren, which
guarded the road to Asmara, the colonial capital

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:46.970
of Eritrea.

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:51.700
In January 1941, the Italian garrison at Keren
desperately needed to buy some time against

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:56.980
the advance of the ‘Gazelle Force’, an
Anglo-Indian motorised and mechanised column.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:12:01.700
Desperate situations call for desperate measures,
and desperate measures require men with courage,

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:06.210
luck, and a moustache so stiff it can deflect
shrapnel.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:10.410
On the morning of the 21st of January, the
troops of the Gazelle Force were taking a

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:12.800
break, brewing tea in the gorge of Keru.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:15.720
Suddenly a shout broke the morning silence:
“Savoia!”

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.130
It was Guillet’s battle cry.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:22.640
Dressed in Arab clothes and riding his favourite
horse Sandor, the Devil Commander was leading

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:25.930
a charge of 250 Eritrean horsemen.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:30.600
The charge cut through a Sikh regiment, flanked
a column of armoured cars and then galloped

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.530
straight towards the British artillery.

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:37.660
Guillet and his men attacked the armoured
plates of the enemy with what they had: pistols,

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:39.290
swords and hand grenades.

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:42.150
As these troops were Anglo-Indian, they described
the grenades as

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:45.500
“flying like cricket balls"
The defenders, taken completely by surprise,

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:47.690
were cut down by swords and shrapnel.

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:51.750
Guillet’s squadrons rode through the column,
regrouped and then launched a second charge.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:55.590
But this time the Gazelle troops were ready,
they trained their guns and fired at point

00:12:55.590 --> 00:13:00.060
blank range, cutting through the galloping
horses, but also causing friendly fire damage,

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:03.210
as the shells exploded amid the Sikhs and
the armoured cars.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:07.860
After a few more seconds the horsemen disappeared
into the network of gorges of the Eritrean

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:08.860
landscape.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:12.890
Guillet had dared the unthinkable: a cavalry
charge against a mechanized column.

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:13.890
Was it worth it?

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:17.820
The raiders’ loss was great – perhaps
half their men – but the damage they inflicted

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:21.350
gave an important breather to their fellow
troops at Keren.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:25.670
The charge at Keru would be the last cavalry
charge faced by the British Army, with many

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:29.970
soldiers declaring it the most frightening
and extraordinary episode of the Second World

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.600
War.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:39.180
A Private War
Guillet knew well that this was a war started

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:42.520
for the wrong reasons, and fought with inadequate
resources.

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:47.760
He continued the fight in two further battles,
Coken and Teclesan, until Asmara fell to Commonwealth

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.060
troops on the 1st of April 1941.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:53.170
Two days later, the now-Captain Guillet made
a harsh decision.

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:58.150
He would disobey orders to surrender to the
British and continue with a war of his own,

00:13:58.150 --> 00:13:59.300
a private war.

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:02.990
With Khadija by his side, he gathered 100
of his best riders and organised them into

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.070
an effective guerrilla fighting force.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:09.600
His aim was to pin down in Eritrea as many
British troops as possible, to prevent them

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:13.110
from joining other theatres in Abyssinia,
Egypt and Libya.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:17.600
The Devil Commander discarded his regular
uniform, donned Arab clothes and even changed

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:22.360
his name to a new Yemeni identity, Ahmed Abdallah
Al Redai.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:26.920
His embrace of Arab customs and asymmetric
war against a powerful Empire would earn him

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:30.760
the nickname of ‘The Italian Lawrence of
Arabia’ after the war.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.880
Over the course of eight months, Guillet and
his guerrillas raided trains, convoys and,

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:35.880
armouries.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.360
They blew up roads, bridges, and galleries.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:42.310
They cut down telegraph and telephone lines,
harassing British communication and logistics

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:43.310
lines.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:47.360
Two British intelligence officers were given
the task to apprehend him: Major Max Harrari,

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:49.300
and Captain Sigismund Reich.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:53.540
Harrari would become a close friend of Guillet’s
after the War, but for the moment he was driven

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:57.770
to get rid of this nuisance: he offered a
reward of 1,000 Pounds - dead or alive.

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:00.560
Yet, nobody reported him to the British authorities.

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.940
Until one day, an Arab farm hand walked into
the British headquarters in Asmara and gave

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:08.780
precise directions as to where they could
find the Devil Commander and his band.

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:13.890
The man pocketed a reward and walked away,
while Harrari’s men set off to finally seize

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:15.510
the stubborn cavalryman.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:17.390
Who was that farm hand?

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:20.880
Well, he was none other than Guillet in disguise.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:24.430
He succeeded in throwing a red herring sending
Harrari on a wild chase, while raising more

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:25.640
funds to his cause along the way.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:26.640
What a sneaky devil.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:27.870
But his luck could not go on forever.

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:30.850
Action after action, the ranks of his band
grew thinner.

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:34.460
Even his faithful steed Sandor was captured
by Harrari’s men.

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:37.440
On one occasion, the farm where he was hiding
was surrounded by the British.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:42.160
In his Yemeni disguise, he jumped from a window
and simply walked away from the encirclement,

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:45.050
ignoring the soldiers’ calls to stop and
identify himself.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:48.990
A quick-thinking Eritrean saved him by telling
the British that that guy was a deaf Arab

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:51.230
peasant and to leave him alone.

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:55.470
With the band reduced to 30 men, and a bad
ankle wound, Guillet decided he should not

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.760
ask more sacrifices from his riders.

00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:01.770
He disbanded the private army, sent Khadija
back to her father and decided to retreat

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:05.400
to Yemen, with just one comrade by his side,
Dhaifallah.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:10.230
Guillet and Dhaifallah travelled to the port
of Massawa and paid a hefty sum to some smugglers

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:11.250
to take them to Yemen.

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:15.180
But the luckiest man in the world was in for
a big surprise: the smugglers did not sail

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.441
across the Red Sea, but instead dumped them
on a desert shore some miles south of Massawa.

00:16:19.441 --> 00:16:24.140
The two adventurers were almost dead from
thirst, when some goatherds approached in

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:25.140
the distance.

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:28.540
They would have surely helped the luckiest
man in the World and his friend!

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:29.540
They didn’t.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:34.060
The goatherds attacked them, beat them senseless,
stripped them of all belongings and left them

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:35.480
for dead in the desert.

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:39.200
Luck finally came knocking, in the shape of
a travelling merchant, who picked the two

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.200
up and sheltered them until they had recovered.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:45.460
With the merchant’s help, Guillet finally
reached the Yemeni port of Hodeida in December

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:46.460
1941.

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:50.630
When questioned, Guillet revealed his identity
and asked for refuge in Yemen.

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:54.220
After a brief stint in prison, Guillet was
summoned to the capital Sana’a, where he

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:58.210
met the local ruler Imam Yahya, who declared
himself a friend of Italy.

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:02.830
The Imam welcomed Guillet in his household,
while the officer made himself useful by training

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:06.439
Yahya’s bodyguards and even treating an
outbreak of cattle sickness.

00:17:06.439 --> 00:17:12.779
Luck was again back on his side, but what
of the war?

00:17:12.779 --> 00:17:18.629
Homecoming
In mid-1943 news reached Guillet of British

00:17:18.629 --> 00:17:23.890
plans to repatriate Italian civilians and
wounded servicemen from Eritrea with a Red

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:24.890
Cross ship.

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:28.510
The reward on his head was still standing,
so he had to sneak on board and travel as

00:17:28.510 --> 00:17:29.510
a stowaway.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.559
In early September 1943, he was finally back
in Rome.

00:17:32.559 --> 00:17:37.460
By now a Major, Guillet demanded men, weapons
and money to resume guerrilla actions in East

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:38.460
Africa.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:42.240
But on the 8th of that month news broke out
of an armistice signed by King Victor Emmanuel

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:43.570
III with the Allies.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:48.190
This armistice effectively split Italy in
two: to put it simply, Fascist loyalists regrouped

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:51.039
in the North and continued the fight alongside
the Germans.

00:17:51.039 --> 00:17:56.169
While monarchists like Guillet and anti-fascists
formed a co-belligerent army in the South,

00:17:56.169 --> 00:17:57.169
siding with the Allies.

00:17:57.169 --> 00:18:01.299
Guillet was assigned to the intelligence services
of the co-belligerents, performing several

00:18:01.299 --> 00:18:05.500
secret missions in Axis-controlled territory,
of which little is known.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:09.470
Of this period of his life we can certainly
claim that he finally married Beatrice in

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:10.850
September 1944.

00:18:10.850 --> 00:18:15.409
Guillet’s last action as a spy took place
immediately after the end of the War in Italy,

00:18:15.409 --> 00:18:17.490
on the 25th of April 1945.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:21.900
A Brigade of the resistance had stolen from
Fascist officials the Imperial Crown of the

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:23.710
Negus Hailé Selassié.

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:28.240
Returning the crown to the Ethiopian ruler
was key to re-establish a peaceful relationship

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:29.249
with that country.

00:18:29.249 --> 00:18:33.429
Guillet staged a daring heist to retrieve
the crown from the partisans and was then

00:18:33.429 --> 00:18:35.570
assigned to personally take it back to Ethiopia.

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:38.820
He took the occasion to meet with Khadija
one last time.

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:43.440
During this tearful reunion, he gave her a
gift – not from him, but from his wife.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:47.340
Surprisingly, Beatrice knew everything about
Khadija and was very understanding.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:52.220
She wanted Khadija to receive a family heirloom
of hers, a beautiful bracelet, to thank her

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:56.759
for taking care of her Amedeo during his dangerous
adventures.

00:18:56.759 --> 00:19:04.399
After the War
On the 2nd of June 1946 a referendum abolished

00:19:04.399 --> 00:19:06.970
the Monarchy in Italy, which became a Republic.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:11.320
Guillet decided he would rather not be an
officer in a republican Italy and retired

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:12.649
with the rank of Colonel.

00:19:12.649 --> 00:19:15.389
His next career would be in the diplomatic
service.

00:19:15.389 --> 00:19:20.110
Due to his knowledge of Arabic, his postings
would be mostly in Middle Eastern and North

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:23.889
African countries, which gave him the occasion
to meet again some of his old friends.

00:19:23.889 --> 00:19:28.140
For example, in the 1950s he was working in
the Italian Embassy in Yemen, where he was

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:29.830
reunited with Dhaifallah.

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:35.520
He returned to Sana’a in September 1962
to attend the funeral of the ruler Imam Ahmad.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:40.070
By coincidence, the same night a coup was
taking place against the new imam, Al-Badr.

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:43.539
Guillet woke up in his room to find Al-Badr
and his followers as they were loading their

00:19:43.539 --> 00:19:44.539
weapons.

00:19:44.539 --> 00:19:47.990
As the conspirators approached he helped them
escape from the window of his room.

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:52.759
As you can see, a diplomat’s life is not
all about entertaining with pyramids of Ferrero

00:19:52.759 --> 00:19:53.759
Rocher chocolates.

00:19:53.759 --> 00:19:56.520
In 1967, Guillet was the Ambassador in Morocco.

00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:59.950
During an official party he was attending,
a coup was launched against King Hassan.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:04.820
When a firefight broke out, Guillet, almost
60, sprang into action and managed to save

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:08.230
several guests from the cross-fire, including
the German Ambassador.

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:12.690
The West German government awarded him the
Grand Cross of the Order of Merit of the Republic.

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:17.110
It is safe to say that the luckiest man in
the World was by no means a lucky charm, neither

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:19.799
to horses nor to Arab monarchs.

00:20:19.799 --> 00:20:22.749
The same retired official who spoke to us
about ‘Black Prince’ Junio Valerio Borghese

00:20:22.749 --> 00:20:26.020
some months ago, also had the chance to meet
Baron Guillet.

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:29.730
He told us that while serving in Morocco,
Guillet liked to take visiting dignitaries

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.899
for a tour of the souk, or market, of Rabat.

00:20:31.899 --> 00:20:36.320
To do so, he would disguise himself as a local
and engage in intense shouting and haggling

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:38.529
matches in Arabic with the vendors.

00:20:38.529 --> 00:20:41.970
On one occasion Guillet and the vendor shouted
so loud that it looked as though they would

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:42.970
come to blows.

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:46.590
Apparently, Guillet had insinuated that the
vendor was the son of a dog and a lady of

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:48.299
the night, to put it mildly.

00:20:48.299 --> 00:20:51.260
Then, suddenly, they burst out in laughter
and embraced each other.

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:52.260
Guillet got away with paying a fraction of
the asking price.

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:56.610
In 1975, following his final appointment as
Italian ambassador in Delhi, Amedeo Guillet

00:20:56.610 --> 00:20:59.519
and his wife retired to County Meath, Ireland.

00:20:59.519 --> 00:21:03.860
After the death of Beatrice in 1990, the former
Devil Commander returned to Rome.

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:08.499
In the year 2000, aged 91, Guillet traveled
to Eritrea for the last time, in order to

00:21:08.499 --> 00:21:12.460
revisit the sites of his adventure with his
biographer Sebastian O'Kelly.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:15.769
He was received with full honours by the Eritrean
government.

00:21:15.769 --> 00:21:23.200
Time moved on and on the 16th of June 2010,
aged 101, after a life of battles and charges,

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:29.100
of bullet, shrapnel and sword wounds, Amedeo
Guillet finally raised the white flag and

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:30.330
gave in to death.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:34.519
Some of our more faithful viewers may have
noticed some similarities with another remarkable

00:21:34.519 --> 00:21:38.350
military leader we have covered, Adrian Carton
de Wiart.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:42.529
Both Amedeo and Adrian
Were born soldiers, always looking for another

00:21:42.529 --> 00:21:45.049
war to fight in
Were cavalry officers

00:21:45.049 --> 00:21:48.169
Suffered a great deal of wounds but never
gave up

00:21:48.169 --> 00:21:52.340
Had a certain degree of madness
Sported enviable ‘taches

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:55.659
Went from military to diplomatic duties
Retired in Ireland

00:21:55.659 --> 00:21:59.940
Adrian was 29 years older than Amedeo and
died eight years before the Devil Commander

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.830
moved to Ireland, so it is fair to say they
never met.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:07.460
But what if for a twist of fate these two
former enemies met in retirement and became

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:08.870
friends?

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:20.159
What shenanigans would they have been up to?

00:22:20.159 --> 00:22:32.559
Post your scenarios or even micro-short-stories
in the comments … and as usual, thank you

00:22:32.559 --> 00:22:32.830
for watching!

